---
import Shot from "../../../components/Shot.astro";
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  docKey="compare"
  title="Viewing and comparing"
  description="Inspect a stacked result in Deptheos: the focus rail, Split and other compare views, zoom, the depth map and Contribution."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Viewing and comparing</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A result is judged at 100%, next to something: a source frame, to see whether it kept the frame's detail,
      or another render, to see which method did better. The best tool for that is Split, a divider you drag
      across the picture with one image on each side.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section id="split">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Split</h2>
      <p>
        With a result on screen, click a frame in the Frames list. The frame is shown alone, brought into the
        result's alignment and color, so the two line up pixel for pixel. Press
        <em>2</em> (or View ▸ Split) and drag the divider: the frame is on the left of it, the result on the
        right, each labeled. Sweep it slowly over an edge and anything the stack lost, doubled or softened
        shows as a jump at the line.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both sides share one pan and zoom. Drag to move around (a right-drag always pans); a mouse wheel zooms
        at the pointer, and on a touchpad two fingers pan and a pinch zooms. Scrolling with ⌘ held (Ctrl on
        Windows and Linux) zooms at the pointer too, and a double-click switches between Zoom to Fit and
        Actual Size there.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot"><Shot name="split-frame" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt="Split at 100%: a source frame on the left of the divider and the stacked result on the right." /><figcaption>Split: a source frame on the left, the result on the right.</figcaption></figure>
  </section>

  <section id="views">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The other views</h2>
      <p>
        <em>Compare</em> in the view bar turns comparing on in the layout you used last, and off again; the
        menu beside it lists Result and the four layouts. The number keys and the View menu do the same.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="choose">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">View</th><th scope="col">Key</th><th scope="col">What it shows</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Result</th><td>1</td><td>The result alone.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Split</th><td>2</td><td>One picture, divided by a line you drag.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Side by Side</th><td>3</td><td>The two next to each other, panning and zooming together.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Top and Bottom</th><td>4</td><td>The same, one above the other, for wide pictures.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Flicker</th><td>\</td><td>One at a time, full size; each press flips to the other, so a difference jumps out.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        While comparing, clicking a frame in the list puts it on the other side, in the layout on screen. Swap
        Sides (<em>S</em>) puts each image on the other side. Esc stops comparing.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="other-side">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What goes on the other side</h2>
      <p>
        The view bar starts with the render shown, "#4 ★". Clicking it goes back to the result alone; the menu
        beside it lists the stack's renders. Next to it is the focus rail, one tick per frame of the stack in
        its order, each as tall as that frame's share of the result, so you can see where along the sweep the
        subject is sharp. Frames the stack left out are amber. Pointing at a tick names the frame and its
        share ("Frame 18 · IMG_0418.DNG · 14%"), and the number beside the rail says which frame is on screen
        ("18/46"). A short tick only means that frame supplied little of the picture: it may still be the only
        sharp one for a small detail.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>A source frame.</strong> Press on the rail and drag along it, scroll over it, or press ← and →
          (or PgUp and PgDn; Stack ▸ Previous Frame and Next Frame) to move through the frames while you watch
          one spot, to find the frame that is sharp there. With the result alone on screen the frame is shown
          alone, lined up with the result; while comparing, it takes the other side, in the layout on screen.
          Clicking a frame in the list does the same.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Another render.</strong> Pick it in the menu beside "#4 ★" while comparing, ⌘-click it in the
          Renders strip, or right-click it and choose <em>Compare with Result</em>. The panes are labeled A (that
          render) and B (the one shown). The swap button in the view bar shows the compared render and compares
          it with the one you had on screen.
          A click on another render while comparing shows that one and keeps comparing; a click on the compared
          render swaps the sides. Your frames and settings stay as they are either way.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A RAW stack's render and a JPEG stack's render can't be compared with each other.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="zoom">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Zoom, and Focus Mode</h2>
      <p>
        <em>0</em> or <em>F</em> is Zoom to Fit and <em>⌘1</em> is Actual Size, 100%, where one pixel of the
        picture is one pixel of the screen; Space, or a double-click on the picture, switches between them. + and −
        zoom in and out a step at a time (25%, 33%, 50%, 67%, 100%, 150%, 200% and so on). With the pointer over
        the picture, Space and the zoom keys keep the spot under it in place. The zoom field in the view bar takes
        a percentage typed in. On iPhone and iPad, a double-tap on the picture goes to 100% at the point tapped,
        and another goes back to fit; pinch to zoom up to 800%, or out past fit to see the picture whole with room around it. Every view, comparison and the depth map shows
        the picture's own pixels at any zoom, the detail coming in as you zoom. Focus Mode (Tab, or View ▸ Focus Mode) hides everything but the
        title bar, the picture and the view bar; Tab again brings the panels back.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="maps">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The depth map and Contribution</h2>
      <p>
        <em>Depth Map</em> in the view bar (<em>M</em>, or View ▸ Show Depth Map) shows, in place of the picture, which frame each part of the result came from. Each area is colored
        by its frame, from the first frame to the last, and the focus rail's ticks take the same colors, so
        the rail is the map's key. Press or drag along the rail to light the parts one frame supplied, with
        the rest dimmed; press that frame again for the whole map. On the desktop, pointing at the map marks
        its frame on the rail, and a click lights it. The map is exact for Depth and Wavelet; for Pyramid it
        shows the frame that was sharpest there. <em>M</em> again goes back to what was on screen before; with
        a frame lit, it shows that frame with Contribution on.
      </p>
      <p>
        Contribution (<em>C</em>, or the highlighter button in the view bar) works while a source frame is on
        screen, alone or beside the result. It shows the parts of that frame the result took as they are and
        turns the rest gray and dim, so you can judge whether the frame was sharp where it was used. It
        stays on as you move to the next frame, so dragging along the rail walks the lit part through the
        subject from near to far. It is the same light as a frame lit on the depth map: <em>M</em> from a
        frame with Contribution on opens the map lit for that frame. It needs a render saved with a record of
        which frame each pixel came from. For Depth and Wavelet that record is exact. A Pyramid result blends
        many frames at every pixel, so the lit part shows where the frame was sharpest, a close guide rather
        than an exact account.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot"><Shot name="depth-map" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt="The depth map lit for one frame, with the focus rail colored to match." /><figcaption>Depth Map: colored by frame, with one frame lit along the focus rail.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="shot"><Shot name="compare-contribution" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt="Contribution in Side by Side view: the parts supplied by the selected frame stay bright while the rest is dimmed." /><figcaption>Contribution: where the result took its pixels from the frame shown.</figcaption></figure>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
